          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em category with duals
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            and/or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object"} duals
            %a{:href => "http://article.gmane.org/gmane.science.mathematics.categories/5807/"}
              %code categories
              list post
            from
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Selinger"} Peter Selinger
          %h2#categories_with_duals_for_objects Categories with duals for objects
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+autonomous+category"} left autonomous category
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                in which every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualisable+object"} dualisable
                on the left.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+autonomous+category"} right autonomous category
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                in which every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualisable+object"} dualisable
                on the right.
            %li
              %p
                An
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/autonomous+category"} autonomous category
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+category"} rigid category
                is a monoidal category that is both left and right autonomous. Note that any braided monoidal category is autonomous on both sides if it is autonomous on either side.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pivotal+category"} pivotal category
                is an autonomous category equipped with a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+natural+transformation"} monoidal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
                from the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity functor
                to the
                %span.newWikiWord
                  double dual
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/double+dual"} ?
                functor. A one-sided autonomous category with such an isomorphism is automatically two-sided autonomous. Although each braided autonomous category has an isomorphism from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                to
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mrow
                            %mo *
                            %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{**}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+monoidal+category"} balanced
                autonomous category is pivotal.
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spherical+category"} spherical category
                is a pivotal category where the left and right
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
                operations coincide on all objects.
            %li
              %p
                A
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tortile+category"} tortile category
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ribbon+category"} ribbon category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/balanced+monoidal+category"} balanced
                autonomous (therefore pivotal) category in which the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twist"} twist
                on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^*
                is the dual of the twist on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %li
              %p
                A
                %strong
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric
                tortile category, or equivalently, a symmetric autonomous category.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                  \-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"} autonomous categories
                do not really belong on this list; being
                = succeed "-autonomous" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                = succeed "-autonomous" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualisable+object"} dualisable object
                = succeed "-autonomous." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} *
            %li
              %p
                Likewise,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed categories
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal categories
                do not really belong on this list, but there is a sense of dual there which should be carefully distinguished from the primary sense here, which is generally stronger. See
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+object+in+a+closed+category"} dual object in a closed category
          %h2#categories_with_duals_for_morphisms Categories with duals for morphisms
          %p One might write something about these too, or put them on a separate page. In the meantime, see the table of contents to the right.
          %p There at least two commonspread kinds of categories with duals for morphisms:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger+categories"} dagger categories
              where each morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X \to Y
              has a
              = succeed "-dual" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo †
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dagger
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mo †
                      %mo :
                      %mi Y
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^\dagger : Y \to X
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"}> groupoids
              , where each morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X \to Y
              has an inverse
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1} :Y \to X
              defined by the properties
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f f^{-1} = 1_Y
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mn 1
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{-1}f = 1_X
          %p Moreover, every category enriched in one of the kind of categories listed above will have a notion of ‘dual’ for its morphisms.
